  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Location and Function
    
    Midway Shelf is — or was — the primary waypoint where cargo and intelligence changed hands along the courier routes connecting the Aurora Stack and the Nightshelf. Its position between the two civilizations made it a natural exchange point, and for generations it served as the logistical spine of Flipper Accord trade: manifests filed, escorts assigned, crates counted and sealed.
    
    A derelict forge occupies one section of the shelf, its origins unrecorded in any source I have located. An Accord Judiciary Holding Station occupies another, equipped with interrogation chambers, holding cells, and a secured evidence vault for processing detentions under Accord enforcement provisions — including Provision 12(c), governing unlawful transfer of protected artifacts. Chambers beneath the shelf serve as neutral negotiation spaces for multi-faction dealings; at least one contains a flooded access channel and shallow pool carved for orca passage.
    
    ## The Seventeen
    
    Seventeen skeletons stand at the shelf in frost-locked poses — flippers bound, beaks open. The canonical account identifies them as couriers who violated the Flipper Accord, left to freeze as warning and precedent. Their bones were later cited as the legal basis for courier inviolability in subsequent treaties.
    
    Seventeen is not a round number, which inclines me to trust it. I have not been able to verify the count from ice-memory at corresponding depth, but the legal citations are real and appear in at least two of the Accord drafts I recovered from administrative ice in the Foundation Depths.
    
    ## Decline
    
    The shelf's operational role has eroded considerably. During the decades when the Midnight Claws, a Nightshelf criminal syndicate, ran diverted fire-cores through sealed crates, couriers carried contraband past the seventeen — knowingly or otherwise. Wartime regulations formalized armed escort assignments alongside routine manifests. The ☽'ofund' Guild, a Nightshelf merchant organization, subsequently suspended courier operations through the corridor entirely, imposing security surcharges on remaining transit. Official manifests now show Coven-stamped crates at nine per cycle — a sharp decline from prior sanctioned trade volume.
    
    Two unregistered incidents preceded the corridor's effective closure: a Cell courier found with a message tube opened and resealed, its documents replaced with an orca bone chip encoding tunnel coordinates, and a ☽'ofund' Guild supply courier found frozen nearby with confiscated fire-core allocation. Neither was formally reported to the Shadow Parliament.
